(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-acid-base-disorders-ii-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Acid-Base Disorders II Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Compared with a simple metabolic alkalosis, how does an added respiratory alkalosis change the pH?","choices":["Same as the simple disorder","Higher than expected","Lower than expected","Normal, at 7.35 to 7.45"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both disorders push the pH up, and the carbon dioxide does not increase as predicted, so the usual partial respiratory offset is missing. The key for metabolic alkalosis with respiratory alkalosis is a pH higher than expected. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What does the Winter formula predict?","choices":["Carbon dioxide in metabolic acidosis","Bicarbonate in respiratory acidosis","Carbon dioxide in metabolic alkalosis","Anion gap after albumin correction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The Winter formula, expected carbon dioxide = 1.5 x bicarbonate + 8 plus or minus 2, predicts the respiratory compensation for a metabolic acidosis, raised gap or normal gap. Carbon dioxide above the range means a concurrent respiratory acidosis; below means a concurrent respiratory alkalosis. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 49, 24, 8"},{"q":"Which of these is one of the intracellular buffers?","choices":["Ammonium","Chloride","Lactate","Hemoglobin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The intracellular buffers are proteins, phosphate and hemoglobin, and together they provide 60 to 70% of body fluid buffering. Bone is listed as a further buffer, and the renal and respiratory system is the most important overall. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A metabolic acidosis shows pH 7.17 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 34 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 12 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). How is the respiratory response best described?","choices":["Concurrent respiratory acidosis","Appropriate respiratory compensation","Concurrent respiratory alkalosis","Concurrent metabolic alkalosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Winter formula: 1.5 x 12 + 8 = 26, range 24 to 28. The measured 34 is above that range. Though 34 is below normal, it is higher than compensation requires, so a concurrent respiratory acidosis is present. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"In alkalosis, which way does potassium shift, and what serum level results?","choices":["Into cells, causing hypokalemia","Out of cells, causing hyperkalemia","Into cells, causing hyperkalemia","Out of cells, causing hypokalemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In alkalosis hydrogen ions shift from inside the cells out to the extracellular fluid, and potassium shifts the other way, from the extracellular fluid into the cells. The serum potassium falls, so alkalosis results in hypokalemia. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which compensation finding points to a mixed acid-base disorder?","choices":["Inadequate or excessive compensation","Compensation within the expected value","Compensation that leaves the pH abnormal","A bicarbonate change in a lung disorder"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Every simple disorder has a predictable compensatory response. When the measured response falls short of the expected value or overshoots it, a second primary disturbance is present: excess or inadequate compensation means a mixed disorder. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 23, 26"},{"q":"An arterial blood gas shows pH 7.40 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 60 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 36 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). What does the normal pH indicate?","choices":["One disorder fully compensated","No acid-base disorder exists","The disorder is still acute","A mixed disorder is likely"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both the carbon dioxide of 60 and the bicarbonate of 36 are abnormal, yet the pH is 7.40. Compensation does not fully correct the pH in a simple disorder, so a normal pH beside abnormal values points to a mixed disorder rather than to no abnormality. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 23, 58"},{"q":"A patient has a serum albumin of 2.5 g/dL (normal 3.5 to 5.5 g/dL) and a measured anion gap of 10 (normal 6 to 12). What is the corrected anion gap?","choices":["10","12.5","15","17.5"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The anion gap is corrected by adding 2.5 for each 1 g/dL that serum albumin falls below 4.5 g/dL. Albumin 2.5 is 2 g/dL below 4.5, so add 2.5 x 2 = 5: corrected gap 10 + 5 = 15, which is raised although the measured gap looked normal. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Why is identifying the type of acid-base disorder useful in a critically ill patient?","choices":["It sets the ventilator settings","Each type has a limited differential","It replaces the history and exam","It confirms the oxygen delivery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acid-base analysis matters most when the patient is critically ill and the cause of the acute illness may not be clear at first. Specific acid-base disorders have limited differentials, so naming the disorder helps reach the definitive diagnosis. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Lactic acidosis from sepsis in the intensive care unit is the classic example of which mixed disorder?","choices":["Metabolic acidosis plus metabolic alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis plus respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis plus respiratory acidosis","Raised-gap plus normal-gap acidosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lactic acidosis is a raised-gap metabolic acidosis, and in sepsis the prevailing carbon dioxide sits below the predicted value: a concurrent respiratory alkalosis. The pH can even look normal. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Carbonic acid, the volatile acid made from fat and carbohydrate metabolism, is eliminated by which organ?","choices":["The lungs","The kidneys","The liver","The bone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Volatile acids from fats and carbohydrates, carbonic acid, are eliminated by the lungs as carbon dioxide. Nonvolatile acids from proteins and ingested sulfates and phosphates are eliminated by the kidneys. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"In a simple acid-base disorder, what does compensation do to the pH?","choices":["Returns it fully to normal","Pushes it past normal","Leaves it unchanged","Moves it toward normal, not fully"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The compensatory response will not fully correct the pH disturbance in a simple disorder; the underlying condition must be corrected to restore pH balance. A normal pH with abnormal values therefore raises the question of a mixed disorder. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which compensatory response is expected in metabolic alkalosis?","choices":["A rise in bicarbonate","A fall in bicarbonate","A rise in carbon dioxide","A fall in carbon dioxide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In metabolic alkalosis the primary defect is an increased bicarbonate, and the compensatory response is respiratory: the carbon dioxide rises, by about 0.75 mm Hg for each 1 mEq/L rise in bicarbonate. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Respiratory alkalosis with a pH below 7.50 usually calls for which treatment?","choices":["An infusion of bicarbonate","Raising the minute volume","No specific treatment","Intubation and ventilation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A mild pH alteration, a pH below 7.50, usually does not require treatment. Management targets the driver, such as pain, anxiety, fever, infection or salicylate overdose, with mindful breathing to help control hyperventilation. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 40, 53"},{"q":"When can a metabolic alkalosis be detected alongside a metabolic acidosis?","choices":["When the carbon dioxide is above prediction","Only when the acidosis has a high anion gap","When the acidosis has a normal anion gap","When the anion gap is low"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Metabolic acidosis with metabolic alkalosis is only detectable with a high-gap acidosis: the change in anion gap is then much larger than the change in bicarbonate, because the alkalosis has pushed the bicarbonate back up. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 30, 50"},{"q":"During recovery from diabetic ketoacidosis, which anion gap pattern is listed for the acidosis?","choices":["Metabolic alkalosis","Normal anion gap","Raised anion gap","Decreased anion gap"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Diabetic ketoacidosis itself is a raised anion gap cause, but recovery from diabetic ketoacidosis is listed under normal anion gap metabolic acidosis, with the loss of bicarbonate. The phase decides the pattern. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 44, 45, 47, 48"},{"q":"Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease treated with diuretics is the classic example of which mixed disorder?","choices":["Metabolic alkalosis plus respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis plus respiratory acidosis","Raised-gap plus normal-gap acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis plus respiratory acidosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is a listed cause of respiratory acidosis, and diuretics cause a saline-responsive metabolic alkalosis. The carbon dioxide is higher than predicted and the pH can be normal. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 29, 32, 53"},{"q":"A metabolic acidosis shows pH 7.48 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 25 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 18 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). What does the carbon dioxide indicate?","choices":["Inadequate respiratory compensation","Concurrent respiratory alkalosis","Appropriate respiratory compensation","Concurrent respiratory acidosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Winter formula: 1.5 x 18 + 8 = 35, range 33 to 37. The measured 25 is below the range, so the lungs are removing more carbon dioxide than compensation requires: a concurrent respiratory alkalosis, which also explains the alkaline pH. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A patient with metabolic alkalosis needs to continue corticosteroid therapy. Which change fits?","choices":["Switch to prednisone","Switch to hydrocortisone","Switch to cortisone","Add a mineralocorticoid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. If corticosteroids are required in metabolic alkalosis, the dose is decreased or the drug changed to one with less mineralocorticoid activity where possible: prednisone or methylprednisolone rather than cortisone or hydrocortisone. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 53, 54, 55"},{"q":"In a raised-gap acidosis, the change in bicarbonate is larger than the change in anion gap, and it equals the change in anion gap plus a rise in chloride. What does this show?","choices":["A coexisting respiratory alkalosis","A simple raised-gap acidosis","A coexisting normal-gap acidosis","A coexisting metabolic alkalosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. When the change in bicarbonate is accounted for by the change in gap and the change in chloride together, a raised-gap and a normal-gap metabolic acidosis are present at once, as in diarrhea with lactic acidosis. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 30, 50"},{"q":"In respiratory acidosis, which medications are looked for and stopped as respiratory depressants?","choices":["Isoniazid and iron","Narcotics and benzodiazepines","Diuretics and corticosteroids","Salicylates and metformin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of respiratory acidosis starts with the underlying condition and a look at the medications: respiratory depressants such as narcotics and benzodiazepines are stopped, since depression of the central nervous system by sedatives is a cause of respiratory acidosis. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 32, 36, 44"},{"q":"A blood gas shows pH 7.40 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 60 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 36 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). What is the best interpretation?","choices":["Fully compensated respiratory acidosis","Fully compensated metabolic alkalosis","A mixed acid-base disorder","No acid-base disorder"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The carbon dioxide (60) and bicarbonate (36) are both well outside their ranges, yet the pH is 7.40. Compensation never fully corrects the pH, so a normal pH with two abnormal values means two opposing primary disorders, here a respiratory acidosis and a metabolic alkalosis. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 23, 29"},{"q":"In a metabolic acidosis, what does a measured carbon dioxide above the predicted value indicate?","choices":["Concurrent respiratory alkalosis","Adequate respiratory compensation","Concurrent metabolic alkalosis","Concurrent respiratory acidosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. With a high or normal anion gap metabolic acidosis, a prevailing carbon dioxide above the predicted value means the lungs are retaining more than compensation allows: a concurrent respiratory acidosis, as in severe pneumonia or pulmonary edema. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 28, 49"},{"q":"Laboratory results show pH 7.56 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 38 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 33 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). If carbon dioxide rises 0.75 mm Hg per 1 mEq/L rise in bicarbonate, which disorders are present?","choices":["Metabolic alkalosis and respiratory acidosis","Primary respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic and respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic alkalosis, compensated"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bicarbonate 33 with a pH of 7.56 is a metabolic alkalosis. A rise of about 8 to 9 mEq/L predicts a carbon dioxide rise of about 6 mm Hg, to about 46. The measured 38 did not rise at all, so a concurrent respiratory alkalosis is present. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 29, 24"},{"q":"Which change raises the anion gap?","choices":["Accumulation of ketoacids","Ingestion of lithium","A fall in serum albumin","A rise in serum calcium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The anion gap increases with more unmeasured anions, such as anionic proteins (albumin), ketoacids, phosphate and lactate, or with fewer unmeasured cations such as calcium, magnesium and potassium. Ketoacids are unmeasured anions, so they widen the gap. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which comparison signals a metabolic acidosis with a concurrent metabolic alkalosis?","choices":["Carbon dioxide below the predicted value","Gap change much larger than bicarbonate change","Gap change equal to bicarbonate change","Gap change smaller than bicarbonate change"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In a high-gap acidosis the change in anion gap and the change in bicarbonate should be about equal. When the gap change is much larger, something has propped the bicarbonate up: a concurrent metabolic alkalosis, as in uremia with vomiting. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 30, 50"},{"q":"An arterial blood gas shows pH 7.34 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 60 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 31 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). With a baseline bicarbonate of 24 mEq/L, an acute rise of 1 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg and a chronic rise of 3.5 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg, which disorder best fits?","choices":["Metabolic alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis","Chronic respiratory acidosis","Acute respiratory acidosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The pH of 7.34 is acidemic with a raised carbon dioxide of 60, so the primary disorder is respiratory acidosis. A 20 mm Hg rise predicts 24 + 2 = 26 if acute but 24 + 7 = 31 if chronic; the measured 31 matches the chronic response. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 11, 24, 34, 35"},{"q":"Which cause produces a raised anion gap metabolic acidosis?","choices":["Renal tubular acidosis","Saline infusion","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors","Ethylene glycol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ethylene glycol is a toxic alcohol listed among causes of increased anion gap metabolic acidosis, with methanol, salicylates and the other toxins. Raised gap causes add metabolic anions, reflect renal failure or come from toxins. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 44, 45, 46, 47"},{"q":"A metabolic acidosis has sodium 140 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 110 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 8 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). After comparing the change in anion gap with the change in bicarbonate, what is the next check?","choices":["The urine chloride","The albumin-corrected gap","The serum potassium","The change in chloride"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Anion gap 140 - (8 + 110) = 22; change in gap 12, change in bicarbonate 25 - 8 = 17. When the change in gap is smaller than the change in bicarbonate, check the change in chloride: if gap change plus chloride change accounts for the bicarbonate change, a normal-gap acidosis coexists. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 50, 30"},{"q":"In acidosis, how does hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen change, and what happens to tissue oxygen delivery?","choices":["Affinity rises; delivery falls","Affinity rises; delivery rises","Affinity falls; delivery falls","Affinity falls; delivery rises"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In acidosis hemoglobin has a decreased affinity for oxygen, so it gives oxygen up more readily and oxygen delivery to the tissues increases. Alkalosis does the reverse: higher affinity and less delivery to the tissues. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"}]};
